mily  hasn't been able to visit him since his  imprisonment  two cases out of many that highlight the  brutality with which those in power in  Iran are dealing with protesters  nevertheless many Iranians agree that  it's worth fighting for  voila let's speak to Daniella zaberry  she is an Iranian activist based in  Germany it's good to have you with us  here on DW news now in that report we've  just witnessed a number of testimonials  of a situation escalating uh in Iran  could you just bring us up to speed on  what activists and protesters have been  telling you from from Iran  they're telling me that the regime is  trying to  um frighten them but what we see in the  in the videos we get what we hear from  the people what we read from the  messages they send us is that their  courage is way greater than the than the  fear and that's what we're seeing also  in baluchista and zahadan happening two  days yesterday it was one day ago  um where the people of baluchistan and  zaidon went out of the streets again  went out to protest again even though  week by week they're getting slaughtered  by the regime and we see that in  Kurdistan we see that in Tehran we see  that everywhere in the country people  are not letting the regime  um frighten them but we are continuing  to see more heavy-handed tactics by the  government what does this mean for the  protesters living there  the government already announced that  there are getting harder and more brutal  against the protesters  um but you see it's about surviving for  the people in Iran it's about the bare  minimum it's about freedom and surviving  and because we know the people know that  they don't have a future with this  regime so that's why they still go on  the streets and protest and also they  know if they stop going out of the  streets then that's it then 18 000  people and more who got arrested will be  hanged just like mohsin shikari was  hanged a few days ago  now more Western sanctions are being  imposed on I